  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "Given that the discrete random variable $Y$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(y)$ shown in the table below, compute $P(1<Y<\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\n| $y$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $F(y)$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.6$ | $0.7$ | $0.8$ | $1.0$ |",
  "worked_solution": "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $Y$ is a function $F(y)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(y)=P(Y\\le y).\n$$\n\nTo compute the probability of a random variable $Y$ over an interval, we can use the rule\n\n$$\nP(a<Y\\le b)=F(b)-F(a).\n$$\n\nHere, we want to compute $P(1<Y<\\frac{5}{2}).$ However, this interval is stated in the form $a<Y<b,$ not $a<Y\\le b.$\n\nSo first, we must convert the interval $1<Y<\\frac{5}{2}$ to the form $a<Y\\le b.$ Looking at the given possible values of $Y,$ we see that $Y<\\frac{5}{2}$ is equivalent to $Y\\le 2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1<Y<\\frac{5}{2}) & =P(1<Y\\le 2) \\\\  & =F(2)-F(1) \\\\  & =0.7-0.3 \\\\  & =0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
